# Camera pricing chooser — delivery brief

## Decision

Build the approved first camera concept on the homepage only. The pricing hub keeps its direct, visible package prices. The homepage chooser becomes a warm, physical camera decision instrument for three buyer needs: property content, filming for a business, and editing supplied footage. A quiet link preserves the existing single-project route.

## Evidence

- `curl -sS -o /dev/null -w 'pricing=%{http_code} homepage=%{http_code}\\n' https://estebanmorenomedia.com/pricing https://estebanmorenomedia.com/` returned `pricing=200 homepage=200` on 2026-10-15.
- At 390x844, the live pricing card visibly shows its starting price; it is the behavior to preserve.
- `components/home-project-path.tsx` currently has a 52px clickable lens-like div plus a second visible chip control. The two controls change the same category.
- `lib/pricing.ts`, `lib/packages.ts`, and `lib/chooser-lane-pricing.ts` are the canonical source of public prices and package labels.

## Acceptance

1. One selector only: three 44px-or-larger semantic buttons integrated into the camera dial/stop rail. No duplicate chip row.
2. On a 390x844 phone, after the chooser reaches the top of the viewport, the selected job, starting price, and package action are visible together without another gesture.
3. The movement is at most 400ms, communicates a selected stop, and respects reduced motion without hiding content.
4. The three existing price shapes remain sourced from canonical helpers: monthly real estate, production-day business work, and per-video editing. No typed price, gear model, turnaround, delivery, audio, lighting, or availability claim.
5. The existing a-la-carte/single-project surface remains reachable as a quiet related route rather than becoming a fourth competing camera mode.
6. EN/ES parity; all lane content remains in initial HTML.
7. Validate targeted tests, typecheck, build, real browser phone/desktop interaction, reduced motion, and raw built HTML.

## Visual direction

Warm metal and cream paper, one terracotta focus marker, and a real mechanical aperture response. The camera is a decision instrument, not a faux technical HUD: no lens focal-length copy, no REC theatre, no neon, no generic camera specifications. The selected option changes the price/scope card immediately beneath the dial.

